\begin_layout Title
Will Posting Salary Information Have an Effect on Individual Productivity?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
November 21, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For many individuals, the topic of yearly salary is taboo.
 Proper social etiquette dictates that asking about a person's salary is
 off-limits because such personal information should be kept private.
 A majority of private companies in the United States follow this norm,
 yet they have different reasons for doing so.
 They believe that posting salary information may cause problems in the
 workplace.
 The posting of salaries could cause strife between the upper echelon of
 earners and those individuals earning a substantial amount less.
 Yet, not only could conflicts arise, but it may give their employees the
 wrong incentives.
 For example, employee A is making $45,000 per year with no opportunity
 for a raise or advancement in the near future, while a comparable associate,
 employee B, is making $47,000 per year.
 If employee A obtains the information concerning the differences in salaries,
 then he may have an incentive to reduce his productivity and effort to
 a level which prevents him from getting fired and/or reprimanded by his
 superiors.
 At the same time, employee B is also conscious of the difference, so he
 does just enough work to set him apart from employee A, and nothing else.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

However, there are still many companies who are electing to eliminate the
 moratorium on salary disclosure.
 First off, employees talk with one another, so salary information is not
 totally private.
 By making this information readily available, it facilitates a level of
 trust between employees.
 Furthermore, keep salaries private perpetuates unfair salaries, which could
 be detrimental for employees and the organization.
 If salaries are kept completely private, then employees feelings of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

unfairness
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 are unable to be addressed directly.
 Finally, companies claim that releasing salary information increases the
 transparency of the company to customers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With my experiment, I hope to discern if there is any change in behavior
 if people can compare how much they earn relative to their counterparts.
 In the controlled experiment, subjects will first be required to complete
 a series of simple math problems.
 The payout they receive will be influenced by their performance, as well
 the performance of the other subjects participating in the experiment.
 So, subjects will be ranked based on their performance on the math problems,
 and then they will be compensated accordingly.
 Note that subjects will be made aware of this payment setup.
 Moreover, personal earnings and productivity information will be the only
 information relayed to the subjects after the first round of math problems
 have been completed.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

After the subjects have been ranked, they will continue to participate in
 several more rounds of math problems.
 Subjects will be paid after each completed round, yet they will only be
 able to receive an amount of compensation equal to their initial payout.
 Also, in order to receive payment at all, they still must complete a minimum
 number of problems, which will be determined based on their past performance.
 The only incentive subjects have for performing more math problems each
 round is the knowledge that they may be able to increase their compensation
 if they show significant improvement in later rounds.
 A later round will be randomly chosen in order to reassess subjects and
 readjust their salary schedules accordingly.
 Let it be known that subjects will be made aware of all of this information.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, the treatment experiment will utilize an entirely different set of
 cohorts, and it will run in the exact same manner as what was just described
 for the controlled experiment.
 The only differnce is that after the first round of math problems, subjects
 will be given the list of rankings and the earnings of their counterparts.
 Will the incentives of subjects change when this new set of information
 is introduced? Will subjects adjust their behavior after they have learned
 the earnings of their peers?
\end_layout

